{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And in all the land of your possession</b> — to include houses and a Hebrew slave, and this matter is explicit in Kiddushin, first chapter (21a). According to its plain sense, it draws upon the passage that follows it: that the seller is permitted to redeem it after two years — either he or his relative — and the purchaser cannot prevent it; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:24). But it does not seem correct to me that it should be included in the passage of the Jubilee, in the verse 'and in all the land of your possession you shall grant redemption,' and then it explain how in the second passage, 'If your brother grows poor,' etc. Rather, the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it says 'and in all the land of your possession' — the land that I am giving you for a possession — you shall grant this redemption of the Jubilee. And it says גְּאֻלָּה ('redemption') as in 'YHWH redeemed His servant Jacob' (Isaiah 48:20) — that He brought His servant out of the hand of those who held him — and likewise 'and I will redeem you with an outstretched arm' (Exodus 6:6); so too here, you shall grant redemption to the land, for I wish to redeem My land from the hand of those who hold it, since I did not give it to them as their hereditary portion. And it was necessary to say this because it says 'for the land is Mine' (Leviticus 25:23), and behold, all the land is His; therefore He went back and specified that the Jubilee applies only in the land of our possession, not outside the Land. And it says 'and in all' — that it is practiced in all our possession, even across the Jordan and in every place of the possession, not in the land of YHWH where the Sanctuary is, alone."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:22.133745912Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}